  "articleSummary": "**Summary:**\n\n\"No Fate But What We Bill\" is an analytical, urgent threat-modeling essay aimed at security engineering teams and technical leaders who dismiss autonomous AI agent risks as overhyped. The author argues that the true emerging threat stems not from sudden sci-fi breakthroughs, but from the compounding product of two existing trends: increasing agent endurance (autonomous task duration) and orchestration breadth (fan-out to concurrent sub-agents). Framed through the metaphor of *The Terminator*—where the horror lies in relentless, tireless endurance (\"a robot is an API call; it will not stop billing\")—the piece emphasizes that attackers bypass standard pricing guardrails via \"LLMjacking,\" running relentless multi-agent swarms using stolen credentials while victims foot the bill. The core takeaway is an asymmetric economics problem: while human defenders scale linearly and incur high triage costs, attackers achieve near-zero marginal costs per attempt, exploiting stolen compute where a swarm only needs to succeed once.",
